ional activity.","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/7816602\">pubmed:7816602</a>  Phosphorylation of the c-fos and c-jun hob1 motif stimulates its activation capacity here we show that the hob1-containing activation domain of c-fos is stimulated by ha-ras in vivo and phosphorylated by a map kinase family member in vitro and that mutating t232 to ala abolishes both functions.","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/12972619\">pubmed:12972619</a>  In a previous study we have observed that exposure of nih 3t3 cells to pdgf or serum leads to c-fos phosphorylation by erk on specific residues, thr232, thr325, thr331, and ser374, within the cooh-terminal c-fos tad we have recently shown that erk phosphorylates multiple residues within the carboxylterminal transactivation domain (tad) of c-fos, thus resulting in its increased transcriptional activity."],"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":86,"s":1,"t":62,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:30274374"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/30274374\">pubmed:30274374</a> AMPK inhibits the mTOR pathway through phosphorylation and activation of tuberous sclerosis protein 2 (TSC2) and causes direct activation of unc-51-like autophagy activating kinase 1 (ULK1) via phosphorylation of Ser555, thus promoting initiation of autophagy."],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates activity"}},{"id":87,"s":1,"t":63,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:30022161"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":["Ser99"],"sequence":["GGIKRTVsEPSLSGLL"],"cell_data":["BTO:0001025"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":[""],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/30022161\">pubmed:30022161</a> We identify the tumour suppressor TET2 as a substrate of the AMP-activated kinase (AMPK), which phosphorylates TET2 at serine 99, thereby stabilizing the tumour suppressor. Increased glucose levels impede AMPK-mediated phosphorylation at serine 99, which results in the destabilization of TET2 followed by dysregulation of both 5-hydroxymethylcytosine (5hmC) and the tumour suppressive function of TET2 in vitro and in vivo"],"i":"up-regulates quantity by stabilization"}},{"id":88,"s":1,"t":63,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:31900833"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0001412"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/31900833\">pubmed:31900833</a> Inactivation of AMPK suppressed the expression of ten-eleven translocation methylcytosine dioxygenase 2 (TET2) in tumor cells.   Compound C-induced AMPK suppression causes downregulation TET2 and FOXP3 expression, leading to death of parental and HQ-selected U937 cells. These results confirm the connection of AMPK with the TET2–FOXP3 axis in modulating the survival of AML cells and suggest that suppression of the AMPK–TET2–FOXP3 axis suppresses the progression of AML and HQ-induced malignant transformation of AML cells. "],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":89,"s":1,"t":11,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:17711846"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":["Ser399"],"sequence":["DNITLPPsQPSPTGG"],"cell_data":["BTO:0000007"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/17711846\">pubmed:17711846</a> Phosphorylation by AMPK leads to the activtion of FOXO3 transcriptional activity without affecting FOXO3 subcellular localization."],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":90,"s":57,"t":64,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:16224021"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":["Ser21"],"sequence":["CWRKRVKsEYMRLRQ"],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/16224021\">pubmed:16224021</a> Enhancer of zeste homolog 2 (ezh2) is a methyltransferase that plays an important role in many biological processes through its ability to trimethylate lysine 27 in histone h3. Here, we show that akt phosphorylates ezh2 at serine 21 and suppresses its methyltransferase activity by impeding ezh2 binding to histone h3"],"i":"down-regulates activity"}},{"id":91,"s":57,"t":65,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:10869359"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":["Ser428","Ser364","Thr440"],"sequence":["GPQRERKsSSSSEDR","EDRNRMKtLGRRDSS","FGQRDRSsSAPNVHI"],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/10869359\">pubmed:10869359</a> We show that phosphorylation of b-raf by akt occurs at multiple residues within its amino terminal regulatory domain, at both the conserved and unique phosphorylation sites. Akt phosphorylated b-raf on s364 and s428 to inactivate its kinase activity b-raf contains three akt consensus sites, table i. One site, ser364 is conserved with c-raf;however, two sites, ser428 and thr439, are unique to b-raf","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/10869359\">pubmed:10869359</a>  We show that phosphorylation of b-raf by akt occurs at multiple residues within its amino terminal regulatory domain, at both the conserved and unique phosphorylation sites. Akt phosphorylated b-raf on s364 and s428 to inactivate its kinase activity b-raf contains three akt consensus sites, table i. One site, ser364 is conserved with c-raf;however, two sites, ser428 and thr439, are unique to b-raf"],"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":94,"s":57,"t":66,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:16926151","pubmed:17964260"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":["","Ser1834"],"sequence":["","MLRRRMAsMQRTGVV"],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/16926151\">pubmed:16926151</a>  We find that suberoylanilide hydroxamic acid stimulates akt activity, which is required to phosphorylate p300 at ser(1834). Akt-mediated phosphorylation of p300 dramatically increases its acetyltransferase activity","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/17964260\">pubmed:17964260</a> Akt1 and 2 promote the association of myod with p300 and pcaf acetyltransferases, via direct phosphorylation of p300."],"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":96,"s":57,"t":67,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:23552696"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":["Ser9"],"sequence":["SGRPRTTsFAESCKP"],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/23552696\">pubmed:23552696</a> Active AKT, a common mediator of cell survival signals induced by radiation through multiple intracellular signaling pathways,11, 12 suppresses apoptosis. AKT positively regulates cyclin D1 expression through inactivation of glycogen synthase kinase 3_ (GSK3_). The AKT-mediated phosphorylation of glycogen synthase kinase 3_ on serine9 decreases its kinase activity for Thr286 of cyclin D1, which inhibits the nuclear export and the cytoplasmic proteasomal degradation of cyclin D1"],"i":"down-regulates activity"}},{"id":97,"s":57,"t":62,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:12782654"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":["BTO:0001103","BTO:0000887"],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/12782654\">pubmed:12782654</a> It was shown recently that akt activates mtor through direct phosphorylation of tsc2 the serine/threonine kinase akt is an upstream positive regulator of the mammalian target of rapamycin (mtor). However, the mechanism by which akt activates mtor is not fully understood. The known pathway by which akt activates mtor is via direct phosphorylation and tuberous sclerosis complex 2 (tsc2), which is a negative regulator of mtor."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":98,"s":57,"t":62,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:10910062"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":["Ser2448"],"sequence":["RSRTRTDsYSAGQSV"],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/10910062\">pubmed:10910062</a> AKT phosphorylated mTOR at two COOH-terminal sites (Thr2446 and Ser2448) in vitro, Ser2448 was the major phosphorylation site in insulin-stimulated or -activated AKT-expressing human embryonic kidney cells.  These results demonstrate that mTOR is a direct target of the PI3K-AKT signaling pathway in mitogen-stimulated cells, and that the identified AKT phosphorylation sites are nested within a repressor domain that negatively regulates the catalytic activity of mTOR.¬†"],"i":"unknown"}},{"id":99,"s":57,"t":68,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:11504915","pubmed:15169778"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":["Ser166","Ser186","Ser188"],"sequence":["RQRKRHKsDSISLSF","SSRRRAIsETEENSD","RKRHKSDsISLSFDE"],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/11504915\">pubmed:11504915</a>  Mitogen-induced activation of phosphatidylinositol 3-kinase (pi3-kinase) and its downstream target, the akt/pkb serine-threonine kinase, results in phosphorylation of mdm2 on serine 166 and serine 186. Phosphorylation on these sites is necessary for translocation of mdm2 from the cytoplasm into the nucleus.Both akt expression and serum treatment induced phosphorylation of mdm2 at ser186.","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/15169778\">pubmed:15169778</a>  Stabilization of mdm2 via decreased ubiquitination is mediated by protein kinase b/akt-dependent phosphorylation.","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/15169778\">pubmed:15169778</a> Stabilization of mdm2 via decreased ubiquitination is mediated by protein kinase b/akt-dependent phosphorylationhere we show that pkb inhibits mdm2 self-ubiquitination via phosphorylation of mdm2 on ser(166) and ser(188)"],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":102,"s":57,"t":11,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:21620960","pubmed:18423396","pubmed:16272144","pubmed:18394876","pubmed:10377430","pubmed:21798082","pubmed:11237865"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation","relocalization"],"residue":["","Ser197","Ser262","Ser319","Ser256","Thr32","Thr24"],"sequence":["","QSRPRSCtWPLPRPE","TFRPRTSsNASTISG","LPRPRSCtWPLPRPE","TFRPRSSsNASSVST","SPRRRAAsMDNNSKF","APRRRAAsMDSSSKL"],"cell_data":["","BTO:0000318","BTO:0002572"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto","gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/16272144\">pubmed:16272144</a>  Foxo4 transcription factor, also referred to afx, contains three putative phosphorylation motif sites for protein kinase b (pkb), thr32, ser197, and ser262, and it is proposed that phosphorylated foxo4 stays in the cytosol and is imported to the nucleus through dephosphorylation to induce target gene expression","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/21620960\">pubmed:21620960</a>  Akt phosphorylates members of the foxo factors (forkhead family of transcription factors), leading to binding of 14-3-3 proteins and cytoplasmic localisation. In particular, akt phosphorylates foxo1 on thr24, ser256 and ser319. Foxo 3alfa and foxo4 are phosphorylated on equivalent sites. In addition, phosphorylation of afx by protein kinase b inhibits its transcriptional activity.","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/18394876\">pubmed:18394876</a>  The phosphorylation of the two remaining akt-dependent sites inhibits foxo6 transcriptional activity","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/10377430\">pubmed:10377430</a>  Here we show that the activation of phosphatidylinositol 3 (PI3) kinase by extracellular growth factors induces phosphorylation, nuclear export, and transcriptional inactivation of FKHR1, a member of the FKHR subclass of the forkhead family of transcription factors. Protein kinase B (PKB)/Akt, a key mediator of PI3 kinase signal transduction, phosphorylated recombinant FKHR1 in vitro at threonine-24 and serine-253. Mutants FKHR1(T24A), FKHR1(S253A), and FKHR1(T24A/S253A) were resistant to both PKB/Akt-mediated phosphorylation and PI3 kinase-stimulated nuclear export.","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/18423396\">pubmed:18423396</a> Akt1/PKBalpha was found to be the major regulator of phosphorylation and nuclear export of FoxO1, whose presence in the nucleus strongly attenuates adipocyte differentiation","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/11237865\">pubmed:11237865</a>  The transcription factor, forkhead in rhabdomyosarcoma (fkhr), is phosphorylated at three amino acid residues (thr-24, ser-256 and ser-319) by protein kinase b (pkb)alpha.Fkhr (forkhead in rhabdomyosarcoma), afx (all1 fused gene from chromosome x) and fkhrl1 (fkhr-like 1) are phosphorylated directly by pkb in cells, preventing them from stimulating gene transcription and leading to their exit from the nucleus","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/21798082\">pubmed:21798082</a>  Akt inactivates protein degradation by phosphorylating and thus repressing the transcription factors of the foxo family, and stimulates protein synthesis via the mammalian target of rapamycin (mtor) and glycogen synthase kinase 3b (gsk3b)."],"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":104,"s":57,"t":11,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:19188143"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":["Ser315","Thr32","Ser253"],"sequence":["QSRPRSCtWPLQRPE","DFRSRTNsNASTVSG","APRRRAVsMDNSNKY"],"cell_data":["BTO:0001130","BTO:0000527","BTO:0000150","BTO:0001271"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/19188143\">pubmed:19188143</a>  Akt-dependent phosphorylation of foxo3a (thr32, ser253, and ser315 for human foxo3) enhances foxo3a/14?3?3 Interaction and promotes foxo3a nuclear export to the cytoplasm, resulting in the repression of foxo3a transcriptional function","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/19188143\">pubmed:19188143</a> Akt-dependent phosphorylation of foxo3a (thr32, ser253, and ser315 for human foxo3) enhances foxo3a/14?3?3 Interaction and promotes foxo3a nuclear export to the cytoplasm, resulting in the repression of foxo3a transcriptional function","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/19188143\">pubmed:19188143</a>  Akt-dependent phosphorylation of foxo3a (thr32, ser253, and ser315 for human foxo3) enhances foxo3a/14-3-3 Interaction and promotes foxo3a nuclear export to the cytoplasm, resulting in the repression of foxo3a transcriptional function"],"i":"down-regulates activity"}},{"id":111,"s":57,"t":11,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:21440011"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/21440011\">pubmed:21440011</a> Phosphorylation of FoxOs by Akt inhibits transcriptional functions of FoxOs and contributes to cell survival, growth and proliferation.The PI3K/Akt signaling regulates cell proliferation and survival in part by phosphorylating FoxOs to promote their interaction with 14-3-3 protein that results in nuclear exclusion and eventual ubiquitin proteasome pathway (UPP)-dependent degradation of FoxOs"],"i":"down-regulates quantity by destabilization"}},{"id":116,"s":69,"t":58,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:11971971"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/11971971\">pubmed:11971971</a> Mapk1 is phosphorylated by map2k1/mek1 and map2k2/mek2 on thr-185 and tyr-187 in response to external stimuli like insulin or ngf. Both phosphorylations are required for activity."],"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":117,"s":70,"t":63,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:25699704"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/25699704\">pubmed:25699704</a> A second group of AML patients (15%–33% of all cases) harbor mutations in either the isocitrate dehydrogenase (IDH) 1 or 2 gene (Shih et al., 2012). These enzymes produce α-ketoglutarate (α-KG), which is required for TET activity."],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":120,"s":56,"t":71,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:9637919","pubmed:19951971","pubmed:9094314","pubmed:21798082"],"mechanism":["chemical activation","relocalization"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto","gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/21798082\">pubmed:21798082</a>  Pip3 acts in turn as a docking site for two kinases, phosphoinositide-dependent kinase 1 (PDK1) and AKT, and the subsequent phosphorylation of AKT at serine 308 by PDK1, leading to AKT activation.","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/9094314\">pubmed:9094314</a>  We tested the kinase in the presence of several inositol phospholipids and found that only low micromolar concentrations of the D enantiomers of either phosphatidylinositol 3,4,5-triphosphate (PtdIns(3,4,5)P3) or PtdIns(3,4)P2 were effective in potently activating the kinase, which has been named PtdIns(3,4,5)P3-dependent protein kinase-1 (PDK1)","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/9637919\">pubmed:9637919</a> In response to PDGF, binding of ptdlns (3,4,5)p3 and/or ptdlns(3,4)p2 to the PH domain of PDK-1 causes its translocation to the plasma membrane where it co-localises with PKB, significantly contributing to the scale of PKB activation.","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/19951971\">pubmed:19951971</a>  PIP3 recruits PDK1 and AKT to the plasma membrane, where PDK1 phosphorylates AKT on Thr308 in the activation loop of the kinase domain."],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":122,"s":17,"t":72,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:25477897"],"mechanism":["post transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/25477897\">pubmed:25477897</a> The down-regulation of miR-29b is thought to promote DNA hypermethylation in AML since miR-29b can directly target DNMT3A, DNMT3B, and Sp1 (a transcriptional regulator of DNMT3"],"i":"down-regulates quantity by repression"}},{"id":123,"s":17,"t":73,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:24708856"],"mechanism":["post transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/24708856\">pubmed:24708856</a> We found overexpression of miR-155 led to increase in cJUN, FOS and TRIB2, and decrease in MEIS1, GFI1, cMYC and JARID2."],"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":124,"s":74,"t":75,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:25477897"],"mechanism":["post transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/25477897\">pubmed:25477897</a> The down-regulation of miR-29b is thought to promote DNA hypermethylation in AML since miR-29b can directly target DNMT3A, DNMT3B, and Sp1 (a transcriptional regulator of DNMT1"],"i":"down-regulates quantity"}},{"id":125,"s":74,"t":76,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:24708856"],"mechanism":["post transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/24708856\">pubmed:24708856</a> In this study, bioinformatic prediction combined with pathway analysis and validation by qRT-PCR revealed that miR-155 expression positively correlates with the expression of the AP-1 genes c-JUN and FOS, which are known to induce myeloid differentiation"],"i":"down-regulates quantity"}},{"id":126,"s":77,"t":62,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:26055960"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/26055960\">pubmed:26055960</a> Our results suggest that activating mutation of FLT3 in AML can lead, to increased expression of miR-155, which then causes down-regulation of SPI1 and CEBPB and consequently causes block of myeloid differentiation."],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":127,"s":78,"t":79,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:23132946"],"mechanism":["post transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/23132946\">pubmed:23132946</a> In human leukemic cells with MLL rearrangements (e.g., MONOMAC-6 and THP-1 cells), we found that ectopic expression of miR-495 could significantly inhibit cell growth/proliferation and increase apoptosis while decreasing cell viability."],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates quantity by repression"}},{"id":128,"s":80,"t":14,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:9710637"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0002882"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["fcortellessa"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/9710637\">pubmed:9710637</a> PLZF expression in 32DG/GM cells is associated with growth suppression and G1 arrest. "],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":129,"s":80,"t":15,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:9710637"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0002882"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["fcortellessa"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/9710637\">pubmed:9710637</a> PLZF overexpression leads to apoptosis. "],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":130,"s":81,"t":14,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:11731443","pubmed:18177723"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["","BTO:0000575"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["","andrea cerquone perpetuini"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/18177723\">pubmed:18177723</a> Cyclin D1 is necessary for proliferation of different cell types, including myogenic cells.","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/11731443\">pubmed:11731443</a>  Cyclin D1 regulates mitogen-dependent progression through G1 phase in cultured cells, and its overexpression in malignant cells is thought to contribute to autonomous proliferation in vivo."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":132,"s":82,"t":83,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:23132946"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/23132946\">pubmed:23132946</a> We then showed that ectopic expression of MLL fusion genes in both human and mouse normal hematopoietic stem/progenitor cells could significantly down-regulate endogenous expression of miR-495 and that the depletion of MLL fusions resulted in the up-regulation of miR-495. Thus, our data suggest that there is an MLL-fusion–mediated negative regulation of the production of miR-495 in hematopoietic cells."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":133,"s":84,"t":55,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:18348712"],"mechanism":["dephosphorylation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/18348712\">pubmed:18348712</a> As reduction of pi3k activity by cd148 or shp-1 [32] is not large (2540%), it is likely that these ptps may function as modulators of the pi3k pathway rather than suppressors."],"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":134,"s":50,"t":85,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:15003515"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":[""],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/15003515\">pubmed:15003515</a> Flt3 Mutation Activates p21WAF1/CIP1 Gene Expression Through the Action of STAT5. Co-transfection of p21 promoter-luciferase constructs with Flt3-ITD plasmid into K562 and BaF3 cells results in the induction of p21 promoter activity and a -692/-684 STAT site is important for the induction. STAT5a binds specifically to this element and Flt3-ITD enhances the protein binding to this site. "],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":135,"s":50,"t":86,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:15626738"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":[""],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/15626738\">pubmed:15626738</a> FLT3-ITD-TKD dual mutants induce hyperactivation of STAT5 and up-regulation of its downstream targets Bcl-x(L) and RAD51 in Ba/F3 cells"],"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":136,"s":50,"t":87,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:26059451"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":[""],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/26059451\">pubmed:26059451</a> … these data suggest that STAT5A positively regulates levels of DNMT3A, resulting in inactivation of tumor suppressor genes by epigenetic mechanisms in AML cells"],"i":"up-regulates quantity"}},{"id":137,"s":50,"t":88,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:15626738"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":[""],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/15626738\">pubmed:15626738</a> FLT3-ITD-TKD dual mutants induce hyperactivation of STAT5 and up-regulation of its downstream targets Bcl-x(L) and RAD51 in Ba/F3 cells"],"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":138,"s":50,"t":17,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:23132946"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/23132946\">pubmed:23132946</a> We then showed that ectopic expression of MLL fusion genes in both human and mouse normal hematopoietic stem/progenitor cells could significantly down-regulate endogenous expression of miR-495 and that the depletion of MLL fusions resulted in the up-regulation of miR-495. Thus, our data suggest that there is an MLL-fusion–mediated negative regulation of the production of miR-495 in hematopoietic cells."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":139,"s":50,"t":45,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:29507660"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0004479"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/29507660\">pubmed:29507660</a> FLT3-ITD is the most frequent tyrosine kinase mutation in acute myeloid leukemia (AML) associated with poor prognosis. We previously reported that activation of STAT5 confers resistance to PI3K/Akt inhibitors on the FLT3-ITD-positive AML cell line MV4-11 and 32D cells driven by FLT3-ITD (32D/ITD) but not by FLT3 mutated in the tyrosine kinase domain (32D/TKD). Here, we report the involvement of Pim kinases expressed through STAT5 activation in acquisition of this resistance. "],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":140,"s":50,"t":14,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:15353555","pubmed:20535135","pubmed:10072077"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["","BTO:0004408","BTO:0000830"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["","miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/20535135\">pubmed:20535135</a>  Specifically, SCF-induced activation of JAK2 in human mast cells has been shown to activate STAT5 and STAT6. STAT5 contributes to mast cell homeostasis, by mediating proliferation, survival, and mediator release. ","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/15353555\">pubmed:15353555</a> Here we report that a persistent activation of STAT5A in human CD34+ cells results in enhanced self-renewal. STAT5A drives the expression of a number of proto-oncogenes and cytokines in human CD34+ cells, as well as a number of erythroid-specific genes, favoring erythroid over myeloid differentiation and providing a long-term proliferative advantage for erythroid progenitors.","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/10072077\">pubmed:10072077</a>  Here, we demonstrate that, while lymphoid development is normal, Stat5a/b mutant peripheral T cells are profoundly deficient in proliferation and fail to undergo cell cycle progression or to express genes controlling cell cycle progression"],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":143,"s":50,"t":15,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:14530308"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0001096"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["apalma"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/14530308\">pubmed:14530308</a> Specific inhibition of Stat5a/b promotes apoptosis of IL-2-responsive primary and tumor-derived lymphoid cells"],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":144,"s":89,"t":90,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:14982881"],"mechanism":["relocalization"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":[""],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/14982881\">pubmed:14982881</a> Furthermore, overexpression of Flt3-ITD led to a partial relocalization of SMRT protein from the nucleus to the cytoplasm. This indicates that shuttling of p65 was necessary for Flt3-ITD-mediated SMRT nuclear export."],"i":"down-regulates activity"}},{"id":145,"s":63,"t":91,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:25601757"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0000670","BTO:0000738"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/25601757\">pubmed:25601757</a>  In this study, we demonstrate that WT1 binds directly to TET2 and recruits TET2 to specific genomic sites to regulate the expression of WT1 target genes."],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":146,"s":63,"t":14,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:25601757"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0000738"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/25601757\">pubmed:25601757</a> Cell proliferation was stimulated by the knockdown of either TET2 or WT1 gene in KG-1 cells, but not additively by the co-depletion of both genes. Collectively, these results suggest that TET2 and WT1 function in the same pathway to inhibit leukemia cell proliferation and colony formation."],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":147,"s":92,"t":27,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:25280219"],"mechanism":["deacetylation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":[""],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/25280219\">pubmed:25280219</a> SIRT1 overexpression was associated with down-modulation of p53 activity in FLT3-ITD AML CD34+ cells. SIRT1 can negatively regulate p53 by deacetylating several lysine sites"],"i":"down-regulates quantity by destabilization"}},{"id":148,"s":93,"t":68,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:27524244"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/27524244\">pubmed:27524244</a> Using MDM2 P1 and P2 promoter-reporter systems, we screened clones regulating MDM2 transcriptions in a p53-independent manner by overexpression. Nine clones from the screening library showed enhanced MDM2 promoter activity and MDM2 expression in p53-deficient HCT116 cells. Among them, six clones, including NTRK2, GNA15, SFRS2, EIF5A, ELAVL1, and YWHAB mediated MAPK signaling for expressing MDM2."],"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":149,"s":94,"t":86,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:22101255"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/22101255\">pubmed:22101255</a> Our results indicated that lnk/sh2b3 constrains expression of bcl-xl and participates in the regulation of hsc homeostasis by maintaining proper responses against various proapoptotic stimuli."],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates quantity by repression"}},{"id":150,"s":94,"t":38,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:18618018"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0002882"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/18618018\">pubmed:18618018</a>  we identified Lnk as a physiological negative regulator of JAK2 in stem cells and TPO/Mpl/JAK2/Lnk as a major regulatory pathway in controlling stem cell self-renewal and quiescence. we identify a direct interaction between Lnk and the Mpl/JAK2 complex that regulates various HSC functions."],"i":"down-regulates activity"}},{"id":151,"s":95,"t":96,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:22252525"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/22252525\">pubmed:22252525</a> The mechanism by which pak1 induced cancer growth might involve activation of jnk  in the non-canonical wnt pathway, frizzled uses galfaq or galfai and gbetagamma dimers to activate phospholipase c (plc), resulting in protein kinase c (pkc) activation and calcium mobilization that regulates the transcription factor nfat, and frizzled also signals through the small gtpases rho and rac to c-jun n-terminal kinase (jnk), which activates the ap1 transcription factor."],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":152,"s":40,"t":41,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:16540375"],"mechanism":["chemical modification"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":[""],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/16540375\">pubmed:16540375</a> Arachidonic acid is transformed into PGE2 via cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes and terminal prostaglandin E synthases (PGES)"],"i":"up-regulates quantity"}},{"id":153,"s":97,"t":98,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:9733788"],"mechanism":["dephosphorylation"],"residue":["Tyr1197"],"sequence":["STAENAEyLRVAPQS"],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["tpavlidou"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/9733788\">pubmed:9733788</a> The sh2-domain ptpase shp-1 binds to and dephosphorylates autophosphorylated egfr and may participate in modulation of egfr signaling in epithelial cells. Reduced 