n a-cdk1 inhibited cdp/cux stable dna binding and prevented repression of the p21(waf1) reporter."],"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":275,"s":126,"t":130,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:16103124"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":["Ser90","Ser86"],"sequence":["LPGSRPGsPEREVPA","TKNGLPGsRPGSPER"],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto","gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/16103124\">pubmed:16103124</a> Moreover, app stabilized tip60 through cdk-dependent phosphorylation","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/16103124\">pubmed:16103124</a>  Moreover, app stabilized tip60 through cdk-dependent phosphorylation"],"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":277,"s":132,"t":35,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:15315962"],"mechanism":["ubiquitination"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/15315962\">pubmed:15315962</a> KIT binds to and induces the phosphorylation of Cbl proteins, which in turn act as E3 ligases, mediating the ubiquitination and degradation of KIT and themselves. Tyrosine kinase binding and RING finger domains of Cbl are essential for Cbl-mediated ubiquitination and degradation of KIT. "],"i":"down-regulates activity"}},{"id":278,"s":132,"t":10,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:19276253"],"mechanism":["ubiquitination"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0001516"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/19276253\">pubmed:19276253</a> Functionally, CBL negatively regulated FMS-like tyrosine kinase 3 (FLT3) activity and interacted with human FLT3 via the autophosphorylation sites Y589 and Y599 and colocalized in vivo."],"i":"down-regulates activity"}},{"id":279,"s":133,"t":134,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:23229552"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0001271"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/23229552\">pubmed:23229552</a> We demonstrate that phf6 is a nucleolus, ribosomal rna promoter-associated protein. Phf6 directly interacts with upstream binding factor (ubf) through its phd1 domain and suppresses ribosomal rna (rrna) transcription by affecting the protein level of ubf"],"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":280,"s":87,"t":5,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:24280869"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/24280869\">pubmed:24280869</a>  HOXA9 is significantly upregulated in both categories of DNMT3A modifications and this has been associated with poor prognosis in AML before (Figure 3d). In fact, almost the entire HOXA and HOXB cluster were significantly upregulated in AML samples with either epimutation or mutation in DNMT3A."],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates quantity by repression"}},{"id":281,"s":87,"t":6,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:28288143"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/28288143\">pubmed:28288143</a> Our results indicate that, in the absence of mixed lineage leukemia fusions, an alternative pathway for engaging an oncogenic MEIS1-dependent transcriptional program can be mediated by DNMT3A mutations.Under these circumstances, those AML patients carrying the alteration in the DNA methyltransferase would undergo a hypomethylation event at the MEIS1 promoter that would lead to the overexpression of this key oncogene in leukemia."],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates quantity by repression"}},{"id":282,"s":87,"t":36,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:26350239"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/26350239\">pubmed:26350239</a> Quantitative real-time PCR (qPCR) was used to investigate the effect of DNMT3A on p18INK4C expression, along with the other INK4 members, including p15INK4B, p16INK4A and p19INK4D. The results showed that the depletion of DNMT3A increased the transcriptional levels of the four members of the INK4 family"],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates quantity by repression"}},{"id":283,"s":87,"t":81,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:19786833"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/19786833\">pubmed:19786833</a> Based on one of these publications, we here showed that the interaction of Dnmt3a with c-myc promote the  speciﬁc methylation of CG  dinucleotides localized in  c-myc boxes  of  promoter  regions  of  CDKN2a, CCND1 and TIMP2  genes.  Acellular  experiments corroborated  and  complemented these results by revealing  that  the  speciﬁcity of  consensus  sequence for  DNA  methylation  of  Dnmt3a is  increased  in presence of c-myc. "],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates quantity by repression"}},{"id":284,"s":87,"t":28,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:27639498"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/27639498\">pubmed:27639498</a> The DNA methyltransferase 3 genes (DNMT3A and DNMT3B) encode methyltransferases that catalyze the addition of a methyl group to the cytosine residue of CpG dinucleotide; therefore they play an essential role in DNA methylation and gene silencing regulatory processes. DNMT3A function is involved in hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) renewal and myeloid differentiation."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":285,"s":62,"t":57,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:21592956","pubmed:15718470"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":["","Ser473"],"sequence":["RPHFPQFsYSASGTA",""],"cell_data":["BTO:0000182","BTO:0000018","BTO:0000132"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/15718470\">pubmed:15718470</a> The rictor-mtor complex directly phosphorylated akt/pkb on ser473 in vitro and facilitated thr308 phosphorylation by pdk1","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/21592956\">pubmed:21592956</a>  Protein kinase B (PKB, Akt) is a Ser/Thr kinase involved in the regulation of cell survival, proliferation, and metabolism and is activated by dual phosphorylation on Thr(308) in the activation loop and Ser(473) in the hydrophobic motif. It plays a contributory role to platelet function, although little is known about its regulation. In this study, we investigated the role of the mammalian target of rapamycin complex (mTORC)-2 in Akt regulation using the recently identified small molecule ATP competitive mTOR inhibitors PP242 and Torin1."],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":288,"s":62,"t":14,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:15829723","pubmed:20508131"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":["BTO:0000007","BTO:0001103"],"notes":[""],"annotator":["","apalma"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/20508131\">pubmed:20508131</a> The mammalian target of rapamycin complex 1 (mTORC1) integrates mitogen and nutrient signals to control cell proliferation and cell size.","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/15829723\">pubmed:15829723</a>  Phosphorylation of mTOR by Akt leads to mTOR activation (40, 52) and the subsequent activation of p70S6K (47). This latter event has great potential importance for the promotion of muscle growth by the IGF-I/Akt/mTOR pathway, because p70S6k is a potent stimulator of protein synthesis that can be activated by increases in muscle contraction"],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":289,"s":135,"t":10,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:24040307"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":["Tyr591"],"sequence":["SSDNEYFyVDFREYE"],"cell_data":["BTO:0005720"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":[""],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/24040307\">pubmed:24040307</a> Pim-1 Kinase Phosphorylates and Stabilizes 130 kDa FLT3 and Promotes Aberrant STAT5 Signaling in Acute Myeloid Leukemia with FLT3 Internal Tandem Duplication[...]Pim-1 inhibition also decreased phosphorylation of FLT3 at tyrosine 591 and of STAT5, and expression of Pim-1 itself, consistent with inhibition of the FLT3-ITD-STAT5 signaling pathway."],"i":"up-regulates quantity"}},{"id":290,"s":135,"t":61,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:25280219"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":[""],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/25280219\">pubmed:25280219</a> FLT3-ITD kinase may regulate c-MYC through STAT5-induced enhancement of PIM kinases (Choudhary et al., 2009), which can modulate c-MYC stability and activity via phosphorylation (van der Lugt et al., 1995s). This is supported by the observation that FLT3-ITD CD34+ cells showed higher PIM activity compared to cells expressing FLT3-WT, indicated by increased expression of the PIM targets including p-BAD (Ser112), p-4EBP1 (Thr37/46), and p-c-MYC (Ser62) (Figure 6C); and by the observation that siRNA-mediated inhibition of PIM1, but not PIM2, expression resulted in significantly decreased p-c-MYC (Ser62), c-MYC, and SIRT1 expression in MV4-11 cells"],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":291,"s":19,"t":10,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:25234168"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":[""],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/25234168\">pubmed:25234168</a> We then found that Hes1 directly bound to the promoter region of the FMS-like tyrosine kinase 3 (FLT3) gene and downregulated the promoter activity."],"i":"down-regulates quantity by repression"}},{"id":292,"s":21,"t":57,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:26084673"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0004136"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["apalma"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/26084673\">pubmed:26084673</a> We have determined that Id1 physically interacts with AKT1, through its C-terminal region, and promotes AKT1 phosphorylation;"],"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":293,"s":136,"t":137,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:31374292"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/31374292\">pubmed:31374292</a> The retinoblastoma binding protein 2 (RBP2) belongs to the KDM5 family, and is also known as JARID1A or KDM5A. We found that histone H3 lysine 4 (H3K4) demethylase RBP2 expression is negatively correlated with BCR-ABL expression, which suggests a regulatory link between these two genes. We also discovered that RBP2 mediates the dephosphorylation of BCR-ABL by directly downregulating PTEN expression, depending on histone demethylase activity, while PTEN targets protein phosphatase activity of BCR-ABL, a phosphatase which directly dephosphorylates BCR-ABL."],"i":"down-regulates quantity by destabilization"}},{"id":294,"s":138,"t":139,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:11404076"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/11404076\">pubmed:11404076</a> We find that Notch 3 IC, like Notch 1 IC, can bind the SKIP and PCAF proteins"],"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":295,"s":60,"t":28,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:15958056"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0000960","BTO:0002062"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/15958056\">pubmed:15958056</a> We thus conclude that TEL is also an accelerator for erythroid differentiation upon cytokine stimulation in human hematopoietic cells. We demonstrated in the present study that TEL accelerates erythroid differentiation induced by a physiological cytokine EPO in human leukemia cell line UT-7/GM."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":296,"s":67,"t":61,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:14563837"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":["Thr58"],"sequence":["KKFELLPtPPLSPSR"],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/14563837\">pubmed:14563837</a> Conversely, overexpression of gsk-3 alpha or gsk-3 beta enhances thr-58 phosphorylation and ubiquitination of c-myc"],"i":"down-regulates quantity by destabilization"}},{"id":297,"s":67,"t":81,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:16504004"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":["Thr286"],"sequence":["EEVDLACtPTDVRDV"],"cell_data":["BTO:0000150"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/16504004\">pubmed:16504004</a> Phosphorylation of cyclin d1 on a single threonine residue near the carboxyl terminus (thr-286) positively regulates proteasomal degradation of d1. Now, we demonstrate that glycogen synthase kinase-3beta (gsk-3beta) phosphorylates cyclin d1 specifically on thr-286, thereby triggering rapid cyclin d1 turnover now, we demonstrate that glycogen synthase kinase-3beta (gsk-3beta) phosphorylates cyclin d1 specifically on thr-286, thereby triggering rapid cyclin d1 turnover."],"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":298,"s":37,"t":140,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:23132946"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/23132946\">pubmed:23132946</a> We then showed that ectopic expression of MLL fusion genes in both human and mouse normal hematopoietic stem/progenitor cells could significantly down-regulate endogenous expression of miR-495 and that the depletion of MLL fusions resulted in the up-regulation of miR-495. Thus, our data suggest that there is an MLL-fusion–mediated negative regulation of the production of miR-495 in hematopoietic cells."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":299,"s":37,"t":17,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:25092144"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/25092144\">pubmed:25092144</a> We showed a strong induction of miR-155 promoter activity by p65. We demonstrate that NF-κB (p65) directly binds to the miR-155 promoter in FLT3-ITD-associated MV4;11 cells."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":300,"s":37,"t":28,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:12130514"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0004730"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/12130514\">pubmed:12130514</a> The transcription factor PU.1 is required for normal blood cell development. PU.1 regulates the expression of a number of crucial myeloid genes, such as the macrophage colony-stimulating factor (M-CSF) receptor, the granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF) receptor, and the granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor (GM-CSF) receptor. Myeloid cells derived from PU.1(-/-) mice are blocked at the earliest stage of myeloid differentiation, similar to the blast cells that are the hallmark of human acute myeloid leukemia (AML). These facts led us to hypothesize that molecular abnormalities involving the PU.1 gene could contribute to the development of AML."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":301,"s":137,"t":137,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:22413754"],"mechanism":["dephosphorylation"],"residue":["Thr382","Thr383","Ser380"],"sequence":["EPDHYRYsDTTDSDP","DHYRYSDtTDSDPEN","HYRYSDTtDSDPENE"],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/22413754\">pubmed:22413754</a> Overall, our results suggest that PTEN autodephosphorylation may be a critical event in this process; thus a major protein substrate for PTEN may be PTEN itself.|Various studies have demonstrated that PTEN is itself a phosphoprotein, and that the major sites of phosphorylation are found in an acidic stretch (DHYRYSDTTDSDPENE) near the C-terminus [1]. This prompted us to consider whether PTEN may autodephosphorylate these sites","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/22413754\">pubmed:22413754</a>  Overall, our results suggest that PTEN autodephosphorylation may be a critical event in this process; thus a major protein substrate for PTEN may be PTEN itself.|Various studies have demonstrated that PTEN is itself a phosphoprotein, and that the major sites of phosphorylation are found in an acidic stretch (DHYRYSDTTDSDPENE) near the C-terminus [1]. This prompted us to consider whether PTEN may autodephosphorylate these sites"],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":304,"s":137,"t":50,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:20596030"],"mechanism":["dephosphorylation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/20596030\">pubmed:20596030</a> The forced expression of pten in the eol-1r cells dephosphorylated akt, erk and stat5 /eol-1r cells showed epigenetic silencing of the phosphatase and tensin homolog deleted on chromosome ten (pten) gene. Exposure of eol-1r cells to imatinib failed to dephosphorylate akt, erk and stat5, although pdgfr? Was effectively inactivated. The forced expression of pten negatively regulated these signal pathways and sensitized eol-1r cells to imatinib."],"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":305,"s":137,"t":47,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:31374292"],"mechanism":["dephosphorylation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0001544"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/31374292\">pubmed:31374292</a> PTEN targets the protein phosphatase activity of BCR-ABL.  PTEN has the same function as PTP1B, which can regulate BCR-ABL dephosphorylation [13]. However, whether PTEN can mediate BCR-ABL dephosphorylation remains unknown. We found that under-expression of PTEN significantly upregulated phosphorylation level of BCR-ABL. In order to verify the mechanisms, co-IP assays were applied, demonstrating the ways in which PTEN and BCR-ABL interact with each other. "],"i":"down-regulates activity"}},{"id":306,"s":66,"t":28,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:20660310"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["amattioni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/20660310\">pubmed:20660310</a> Switch to beta-catenin/p300-mediated gene expression is an essential first step in initiating normal cellular differentiation"],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":307,"s":141,"t":129,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:17379597"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0002181"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/17379597\">pubmed:17379597</a> BCoR-L1 also interacts with the CtBP corepressor through a CtBP-interacting motif in its amino terminus. Furthermore, BCoR-L1 is located on the E-cadherin promoter, a known CtBP-regulated promoter, and represses the E-cadherin promoter activity in a reporter assay."],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":308,"s":36,"t":3,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:8891723"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/8891723\">pubmed:8891723</a> The first group, including p16ink4a, p15ink4b,p18ink4cand p19ink4d, is specific for the g1 cdks,cdk4and cdk6, inhibiting the kinase activity of cyclin d/cdk4-cdk6 complexes on prb."],"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":309,"s":36,"t":14,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:7972006"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0000176"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":[""],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/7972006\">pubmed:7972006</a> Transfection of the p16INK4 cDNA expression vector into carcinoma cells inhibits their colony-forming efficiency and the p16INK4 expressing cells are selected against with continued passage in vitro. These results are consistent with the hypothesis that p16INK4 is a tumor-suppressor protein and that genetic and epigenetic abnormalities in genes controlling the G1 checkpoint can lead to both escape from senescence and cancer formation."],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":310,"s":142,"t":63,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:28516957"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/28516957\">pubmed:28516957</a> Interestingly, this identified a number of genes known to promote leukemogenesis (either alone or in the context of AML1-ETO leukemia) as differentially expressed by ASXL2 loss. These include downregulation of TET2 as well as NOTCH2 with ASXL2 loss in human AML1-ETO-expressing cells, downregulation of which have been previously shown to functionally promote myeloid leukemogenesis when altered in expression"],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":311,"s":36,"t":27,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:12091906"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["apalma"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/12091906\">pubmed:12091906</a> P14/p19 ARF functions by antagonizing MDM2 and thereby stabilizing p53 (refs. 17,18). Thus, loss of p14/p19ARF impairs p53-mediated growth arrest and/or apoptosis in response to activated oncogenes"],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates quantity by stabilization"}},{"id":312,"s":36,"t":68,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:23416275"],"mechanism":["relocalization"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["fstefani"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/23416275\">pubmed:23416275</a> We propose that p14(arf) increases the binding of p53-mdm2 complexes to chromatin, thereby limiting the access of protein deacetylases to p53."],"i":"down-regulates activity"}},{"id":313,"s":59,"t":15,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:1286168"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/1286168\">pubmed:1286168</a> Bcl-2 functions to inhibit apoptosis in a variety of in vitro and in vivo experiments, suggesting interference with a central mechanism of apoptosis"],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":314,"s":115,"t":61,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:15103331"],"mechanism":["ubiquitination"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0000007"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/15103331\">pubmed:15103331</a> We now show that the F-box protein Fbw7 interacts with and thereby destabilizes c-Myc in a manner dependent on phosphorylation of MB1"],"i":"down-regulates quantity by destabilization"}},{"id":315,"s":115,"t":61,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:20852628"],"mechanism":["ubiquitination"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/20852628\">pubmed:20852628</a> We now show that the F-box protein Fbw7 interacts with and thereby destabilizes c-Myc in a manner dependent on phosphorylation of MB1. Whereas wild-type Fbw7 promoted c-Myc turnover in cells, an Fbw7 mutant lacking the F-box domain delayed it."],"i":"down-regulates quantity"}},{"id":316,"s":143,"t":10,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:12681969","pubmed:10080542"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["","BTO:0001271"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/10080542\">pubmed:10080542</a>  Flt3 ligand (fl) is an early-acting potent co-stimulatory cytokine that regulates proliferation and differentiation of a number of blood cell lineages. Its receptor flt3/flk2 belongs to class iii receptor tyrosine kinases that also include the receptors for colony-stimulating factor 1","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/12681969\">pubmed:12681969</a> Flt3 is activated by binding of its natural flt3-ligand (flt3-l),"],"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":318,"s":117,"t":35,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:15315962"],"mechanism":["ubiquitination"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/15315962\">pubmed:15315962</a> KIT binds to and induces the phosphorylation of Cbl proteins, which in turn act as E3 ligases, mediating the ubiquitination and degradation of KIT and themselves. Tyrosine kinase binding and RING finger domains of Cbl are essential for Cbl-mediated ubiquitination and degradation of KIT. "],"i":"down-regulates activity"}},{"id":319,"s":117,"t":10,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:19276253"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0001516"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":[""],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/19276253\">pubmed:19276253</a> Functionally, CBL negatively regulated FMS-like tyrosine kinase 3 (FLT3) activity and interacted with human FLT3 via the autophosphorylation sites Y589 and Y599 and colocalized in vivo."],"i":"down-regulates activity"}},{"id":320,"s":3,"t":24,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:21059642"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":["Ser276"],"sequence":["VHPATPIsPGRASGM"],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":["The effect has been demonstrated using Q01196-8"],"annotator":["gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/21059642\">pubmed:21059642</a> Phosphorylation of runx1 on ser-303 by cdks leads its ubiquitin-mediated degradation during g2/m (19). We developed additional evidence that cdks phosphorylate ser-303 and found that ser-48 and ser-424 are also substrates of cdk1/cyclin b and cdk6/cyclin d3. Moreover, we demonstrated that phosphorylation of ser-48, ser-303, and ser-424 strengthens the ability of runx1 to activate transcription and to stimulate proliferation of the ba/f3 hematopoietic cell line (20)."],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":321,"s":23,"t":144,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:27856324"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/27856324\">pubmed:27856324</a> Previously, we found that MLL-AF4 binds to the BCL-2 gene and directly activates it through DOT1L recruitment and increased H3K79me2/3 levels. MLL-AF4 directly controls the active transcription of both BCL-2 and MCL-1 […]. Of all the BCL-2 family members, only BCL-2 and MCL-1 are directly activated by MLL-AF4."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":322,"s":23,"t":5,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:20854876"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/20854876\">pubmed:20854876</a> Inhibition of EAP components pTEFb and Dot1l show that both contribute significantly to activation of Hoxa9 and Meis1 expression. EAP is dynamically associated with the Hoxa9 and Meis1 loci in hematopoietic cells and rapidly dissociates during induction of differentiation. In the presence of MLL fusion proteins, its dissociation is prevented."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":323,"s":23,"t":6,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:20854876"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/20854876\">pubmed:20854876</a> Inhibition of EAP components pTEFb and Dot1l show that both contribute significantly to activation of Hoxa9 and Meis1 expression. EAP is dynamically associated with the Hoxa9 and Meis1 loci in hematopoietic cells and rapidly dissociates during induction of differentiation. In the presence of MLL fusion proteins, its dissociation is prevented."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":324,"s":23,"t":59,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:27856324"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/27856324\">pubmed:27856324</a> Previously, we found that MLL-AF4 binds to the BCL-2 gene and directly activates it through DOT1L recruitment and increased H3K79me2/3 levels. MLL-AF4 directly controls the active transcription of both BCL-2 and MCL-1 […]. Of all the BCL-2 family members, only BCL-2 and MCL-1 are directly activated by MLL-AF4."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":325,"s":23,"t":61,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:26199140"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0001939"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":["1"],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/26199140\">pubmed:26199140</a> Our data suggest that the c-Myc-dependent transcriptional switch is modulated by DOT1L, as in the presence of DOT1L c-Myc preferentially forms an active complex with p300 rather than a repressive complex containing HDAC1 and DNMT1"],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":326,"s":86,"t":15,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:9393856"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0003328"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["fcortellessa"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/9393856\">pubmed:9393856</a>  Bcl-xL Expression Prevents Cytochrome c Redistribution and Subsequent Mitochondrial Depolarization during Apoptosis. Bcl-xL expression prevented both cytochrome c redistribution and mitochondrial membrane depolarization. In contrast, zVAD treatment could not prevent either cytochrome c redistribution or mitochondrial membrane depolarization in control transfectants withdrawn from IL-3. Thus, cytochrome c redistribution from mitochondria is an early apoptotic event that precedes mitochondrial membrane depolarization. Bcl-xL expression functions to inhibit both of these events. In at least some forms of cell death, the ability of Bcl-xL to regulate these mitochondrial events cannot be mimicked by caspase inhibition"],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":327,"s":131,"t":103,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:24316979"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/24316979\">pubmed:24316979</a> We demonstrate that CUX1 deficiency activates phosphoinositide 3-kinase (PI3K) signaling through direct transcriptional downregulation of the PI3K inhibitor PIK3IP1 (phosphoinositide-3-kinase interacting protein 1), leading to increased tumor growth and susceptibility to PI3K-AKT inhibition."],"i":"down-regulates quantity by repression"}},{"id":328,"s":42,"t":14,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:23645839","pubmed:18697834"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["Simone Vumbaca","apalma"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/18697834\">pubmed:18697834</a>   we showed that β-catenin, a key component of the canonical Wnt-signalling cascade, is present in quiescent satellite cells in the inactive form, but subsequently becomes activated following satellite-cell activation. This observation suggests that the proliferation initiated by the Wnt-signalling cascade does not have to rely on transcription of β-catenin, but rather on activation of this protein, which is already present within the quiescent satellite cells.","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/23645839\">pubmed:23645839</a> For example, prostaglandin E2 (PGE2), 1 of the major metabolites downstream of both COX-1 and COX-2, has been shown to activate β-catenin–dependent signaling in hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) and promote HSC expansion"],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":330,"s":65,"t":69,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:8413257","pubmed:21900390"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu","lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/21900390\">pubmed:21900390</a> BRAFV600E has been shown to initiate thyroid follicular cell transformation. The BRAFV600E mutation disrupts the hydrophobic interaction, enabling the BRAF kinase to fold into a catalytically active formation, resulting in an almost 500-fold increase in kinase activity. Mutant BRAF can dimerize and activate MEK without Ras activation.","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/8413257\">pubmed:8413257</a>  Raf-1 phosphorylation of MEK activated it, as judged by its ability to stimulate the phosphorylation of myelin basic protein by glutathione S-transferase-ERK1."],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":331,"s":65,"t":69,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:8668348"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/8668348\">pubmed:8668348</a> We show that, consequently, b-raf interacts with mek-1 and mek-2 with a better affinity than does c-raf-1, thus strengthening the notion that b-raf is a stronger mek activator than c-raf-l."],"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":333,"s":65,"t":58,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:21900390"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/21900390\">pubmed:21900390</a> RAF, a cytoplasmic serine-threonine protein kinase, is a member of the RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK cell-signaling pathway [also known as the MAP kinase (MAPK) pathway], and it plays an essential role in mediating cellular differentiation, proliferation, senescence, and survival in response to extracellular cues. Raf phosphorylates and activates MAP-ERK kinase (MEK), which phosphorylates and activates extracellular signal-regulated kinase (ERK)."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":334,"s":46,"t":14,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:23725574"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0002203","BTO:0002207","BTO:0000018","BTO:0002418","BTO:0002552"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/23725574\">pubmed:23725574</a> Our data suggested that increased expression of BAD enhance apoptosis and has negative influence on cell proliferation and tumor growth in NSCLC. Bad is a new potential target for tumor interventions."],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":335,"s":46,"t":15,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:23725574"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0002203","BTO:0002207","BTO:0000018","BTO:0002418","BTO:0002552"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/23725574\">pubmed:23725574</a> Our data suggested that increased expression of BAD enhance apoptosis and has negative influence on cell proliferation and tumor growth in NSCLC. Bad is a new potential target for tumor interventions."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":336,"s":100,"t":5,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:22897849"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/22897849\">pubmed:22897849</a>  ASXL1 siRNA in human primary CD34+ cells form cord blood results in upregulation of HOXA5 and HOXA9 with ASXL1 knockdown (KD) as revealed by quantitative real-time PCR"],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates quantity by repression"}},{"id":337,"s":100,"t":36,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:29967380"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/29967380\">pubmed:29967380</a> Modeling ASXL1 mutation revealed impaired hematopoiesis caused by derepression of p16Ink4a through aberrant PRC1-mediated histone modification. These results indicated that loss of protein interaction between Asxl1 mutant and Bmi1 affected the activity of PRC1, and subsequent derepression of p16Ink4a by aberrant histone ubiquitination could induce cellular senescence, resulting in low-risk MDS-like phenotypes in Asxl1G643fs/+ mice."],"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":338,"s":100,"t":108,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:16606617"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0000567"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/16606617\">pubmed:16606617</a> In this study, we demonstrate that mammalian ASXL1 interacts with the AF-2 AD core of RAR (and RXR) through a novel, promiscuous NR box (LVMQLL) and enhances transcriptional activity of the receptors in certain cells."],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":339,"s":100,"t":101,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:16606617"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0000567"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/16606617\">pubmed:16606617</a> Therefore, ASXL1, a vertebrate PcG/TrxG protein, may mediate RA-regulated cell growth by modulating RAR activity.Finally, the ASXL1-induced accumulation of acetylated H3 may enhance the RAR-mediated transcriptional activity. In this study, we demonstrate that mammalian ASXL1 interacts with the AF-2 AD core of RAR (and RXR) through a novel, promiscuous NR box (LVMQLL) and enhances transcriptional activity of the receptors in certain cells."],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":340,"s":100,"t":101,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:16606617"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/16606617\">pubmed:16606617</a> We also show that ASXL1 associates specifically with SRC-1 and cooperates synergistically in the transcriptional activation. Further data indicated that the transactivation domain (AD; amino acids 300–655) of ASXL1, newly defined in this study, interacts with the C-terminal AD2 (amino acids 1217–1441) of SRC-1, suggesting that one AD cooperates with the other AD in transcriptional activation by RAR."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":341,"s":100,"t":99,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:16606617"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/16606617\">pubmed:16606617</a> We also show that ASXL1 associates specifically with SRC-1 and cooperates synergistically in the transcriptional activation. Further data indicated that the transactivation domain (AD; amino acids 300–655) of ASXL1, newly defined in this study, interacts with the C-terminal AD2 (amino acids 1217–1441) of SRC-1, suggesting that one AD cooperates with the other AD in transcriptional activation by RAR."],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":342,"s":134,"t":14,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:15169904"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0002882"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/15169904\">pubmed:15169904</a> Pescadillo (PES1) and the upstream binding factor (UBF1) play a role in ribosome biogenesis, which regulates cell size, an important component of cell proliferation. We have investigated the effects of PES1 and UBF1 on the growth and differentiation of cell lines derived from 32D cells, an interleukin-3 (IL-3)-dependent murine myeloid cell line. Parental 32D cells and 32D IGF-IR cells (expressing increased levels of the type 1 insulin-like growth factor I [IGF-I] receptor [IGF-IR]) do not express insulin receptor substrate 1 (IRS-1) or IRS-2. 32D IGF-IR cells differentiate when the cells are shifted from IL-3 to IGF-I. Ectopic expression of IRS-1 inhibits differentiation and transforms 32D IGF-IR cells into a tumor-forming cell line. We found that PES1 and UBF1 increased cell size and/or altered the cell cycle distribution of 32D-derived cells but failed to make them IL-3 independent. PES1 and UBF1 also failed to inhibit the differentiation program initiated by the activation of the IGF-IR, which is blocked by IRS-1. 32D IGF-IR cells expressing PES1 or UBF1 differentiate into granulocytes like their parental cells. In contrast, PES1 and UBF1 can transform mouse embryo fibroblasts that have high levels of endogenous IRS-1 and are not prone to differentiation. Our results provide a model for one of the theories of myeloid leukemia, in which both a stimulus of proliferation and a block of differentiation are required for leukemia development."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":343,"s":123,"t":110,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:10207047"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0000776"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/10207047\">pubmed:10207047</a> The results indicate that ship, shc, and grb2 form a ternary complex in stimulated b cells, with grb2 stabilizing the interaction between shc and ship. The interactions between shc, grb2, and ship are therefore analogous to the interactions between shc, grb2, and sos. Shc and grb2 may help to localize ship to the cell membrane, regulating ship's inhibitory function following bcr stimulation."],"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":344,"s":123,"t":58,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:17673906"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0000944"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/17673906\">pubmed:17673906</a> We report that upon TGF__ stimulation, the activated TGF__ type I receptor (T_RI) recruits and directly phosphorylates ShcA proteins on tyrosine and serine. This dual phosphorylation results from an intrinsic T_RI tyrosine kinase activity that complements its well_defined serine_threonine kinase function. TGF___induced ShcA phosphorylation induces ShcA association with Grb2 and Sos, thereby initiating the well_characterised pathway linking receptor tyrosine kinases with Erk MAP kinases."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":345,"s":116,"t":49,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:11560935"],"mechanism":["guanine nucleotide exchange factor"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0000938"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/11560935\">pubmed:11560935</a> Sos and Ras-GRF are two families of guanine nucleotide exchange factors that activate Ras proteins in cells. Sos proteins are ubiquitously expressed and are activated in response to cell-surface tyrosine kinase stimulation Sos1 and Ras-GRF1 activate the Ras proteins Ha-Ras, N-Ras, and Ki-Ras"],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":346,"s":116,"t":107,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:25624485"],"mechanism":["guanine nucleotide exchange factor"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":["Ras proteins bind GDP/GTP and possess intrinsic GTPase activity."],"annotator":["gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/25624485\">pubmed:25624485</a> Because the KRAS-GDP to KRAS-GTP transition catalyzed by the GEF, son of sevenless 1 (SOS1), represents the rate-limiting step for nucleotide exchange, disrupting the activating SOS1/KRAS protein interaction has also been the focus of drug development efforts "],"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":347,"s":145,"t":35,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:1698556"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/1698556\">pubmed:1698556</a> We have also provided biological and physical evidence that scf is a ligand for the c-kit receptor."],"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":348,"s":145,"t":35,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:17259966"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/17259966\">pubmed:17259966</a> The most relevant and still unique mast-cell growth factor is SCF, which is the ligand of KIT, a receptor with tyrosine-kinase activity that is expressed on the surface of all human and murine mast cells"],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":349,"s":128,"t":14,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:25194572","pubmed:18177723"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0002314"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu","lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/18177723\">pubmed:18177723</a> Altogether, these data demonstrate that IL-6 loss results in deficient STAT3 signaling in activated satellite cells, leading to their reduced proliferation and myogenic progression, and highlight the major role played by the IL-6/STAT3 axis in controlling these processes during compensatory hypertrophy.","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/25194572\">pubmed:25194572</a>  STAT3 signaling controls satellite cell expansion and skeletal muscle repair"],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":351,"s":91,"t":87,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:23042785"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/23042785\">pubmed:23042785</a> Here, we show that Wilms' tumour 1 (WT1), a developmental master regulator that can also act as a tumour suppressor or oncoprotein, transcriptionally regulates the de novo DNA methyltransferase 3A (DNMT3A) and that cellular WT1 levels can influence DNA methylation of gene promoters genome-wide. we demonstrate that depletion of WT1 by short-interfering RNAs leads to reduced DNMT3A in Wilms' tumour cells and human embryonal kidney-derived cell lines. Chromatin immunoprecipitation assays demonstrate WT1 recruitment to the DNMT3A promoter region and reporter assays confirm that WT1 directly transactivates DNMT3A expression."],"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":352,"s":91,"t":14,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:25601757"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0000738"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/25601757\">pubmed:25601757</a> Cell proliferation was stimulated by the knockdown of either TET2 or WT1 gene in KG-1 cells, but not additively by the co-depletion of both genes. Collectively, these results suggest that TET2 and WT1 function in the same pathway to inhibit leukemia cell proliferation and colony formation."],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":353,"s":29,"t":29,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:11283671"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0001056"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["apalma"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/11283671\">pubmed:11283671</a> Here, we demonstrate that C/EBPα indeed activates its promoter in transient transfection assays in myeloid cells."],"i":"up-regulates quantity"}},{"id":354,"s":29,"t":37,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:14592841"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":[""],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/14592841\">pubmed:14592841</a> Activation of C/EBPα induces PU.1 expression, cell cycle arrest, and differentiation in 32D cells expressing FLT3/ITD"],"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":355,"s":29,"t":61,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:12032779"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/12032779\">pubmed:12032779</a>  Several different transcription factors have been implicated in the down-regulation of c-myc expression during differentiation, including C/EBPalpha, CTCF, BLIMP-1, and RFX1. "],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates quantity by repression"}},{"id":356,"s":29,"t":109,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:24183681"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["apalma"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/24183681\">pubmed:24183681</a> In summary, our data demonstrate that C/EBPα negatively regulates Sox4 transcription via direct DNA-binding."],"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":357,"s":29,"t":28,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:16319681"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0004730"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/16319681\">pubmed:16319681</a> The transcription factor C/EBPalpha controls differentiation and proliferation in normal granulopoiesis in a stage-specific manner. Loss of C/EBPalpha function in myeloid cells in vitro and in vivo leads to a block to myeloid differentiation similar to that which is observed in malignant cells from patients with acute myeloid leukemia. The finding of C/EBPalpha alterations in subgroups of acute myeloid leukemia patients suggests a direct link between critically decreased C/EBPalpha function and the development of the disorder."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":358,"s":121,"t":42,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:15735151"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["amattioni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/15735151\">pubmed:15735151</a> Activated DVL binds and inhibits the phosphorylation of beta-catenin by GSK3B, blocking beta-catenin degradation so that beta catenin accumulates and translocates to the nucleus, where it interacts with the t cell specific factor (tcf)/lymphoid enhancer binding factor 1 (lef-1) transcription factor and induces the transcription of target genes such as c-jun, c-myc, and cyclin d1."],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":359,"s":64,"t":5,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:20565746"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/20565746\">pubmed:20565746</a> These data support the proposed regulatory impact of particular PRC2-proteins in expression of HOXA9 and HOXA10 in NK/T-cells. In mammalian cells knockdown of PRC2 components EZH2 or PHF1 led to upregulated HOXA gene expression."],"i":"down-regulates quantity by repression"}},{"id":360,"s":139,"t":19,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:19165418"],"mechanism":["transcriptional regulation"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["lperfetto"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/19165418\">pubmed:19165418</a> Several lines of evidence have suggested that these genes are indeed direct notch target genes: a) the promoters of hes1, hes5 and hes7 as well as hey1, hey2 and heyl subfamily of hes, related with yrpw motif) can be activated by a constitutive active form of notch1."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates quantity by expression"}},{"id":361,"s":139,"t":28,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:18342499"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["flangone"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/18342499\">pubmed:18342499</a> Genetic ablation or activation of the pathway reveals that Notch signalling promotes differentiation of the hair follicle, sebaceous gland and interfollicular epidermal lineages"],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":362,"s":6,"t":14,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:19109563"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0001271"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/19109563\">pubmed:19109563</a> These results show that MEIS1 expression is important for MLL-rearranged leukemias and suggest that MEIS1 promotes cell-cycle entry.Flow cytometric analysis of PI-stained nuclei showed that Meis1 knockdown led to a cell-cycle arrest in the G0/G1 phase."],"direct":false,"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":363,"s":6,"t":28,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:14701735"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0000725"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/14701735\">pubmed:14701735</a> Here we demonstrate that MLL-ENL immortalizes cells mainly through inducing a reversible block on myeloid differentiation that is dependent on upregulation of Hoxa9 and Meis1 and that enforced expression of these two genes is sufficient to substitute for MLL-ENL function."],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":364,"s":6,"t":15,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:19109563"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0001271"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/19109563\">pubmed:19109563</a> To discern the mechanisms by which Meis1 inhibition leads to reduced cell growth, we performed cell-cycle and apoptosis analyses.Meis1 knockdown also resulted in increased apoptosis, as evidenced by increased uptake of PI and a stain for activated caspases (CaspaTag) by M26-transduced cells compared with control cells. These results indicate that Meis1 is required for proliferation and survival of 4166 leukemia cells."],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":365,"s":5,"t":6,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:9343407"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":["2"],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/9343407\">pubmed:9343407</a> We now show that the Hoxa-9 protein physically interacts with Meis1 proteins.  Hox proteins from the other AbdB-like paralogs, Hoxa-10, Hoxa-11, Hoxd-12, and Hoxb-13, also form DNA binding complexes with Meis1b. DNA binding complexes formed by Meis1 with Hox proteins dissociate much more slowly than DNA complexes with Meis1 alone, suggesting that Hox proteins stabilize the interactions of Meis1 proteins with their DNA targets."],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":366,"s":5,"t":28,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:14701735"],"mechanism":[""],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":["BTO:0000725"],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["irozzo"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/14701735\">pubmed:14701735</a> Here we demonstrate that MLL-ENL immortalizes cells mainly through inducing a reversible block on myeloid differentiation that is dependent on upregulation of Hoxa9 and Meis1 and that enforced expression of these two genes is sufficient to substitute for MLL-ENL function."],"direct":false,"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":367,"s":146,"t":70,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:29090344"],"mechanism":["chemical modification"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/29090344\">pubmed:29090344</a> Two of the most commonly mutated genes in AML encode for two isoforms of isocitrate dehydrogenase (IDH), IDH1 and IDH2. IDH1 and IDH2 are two isoforms of isocitrate dehydrogenase that perform crucial roles in cellular metabolism. Somatic mutations in either of these two genes impart a neomorphic enzymatic activity upon the encoded enzymes resulting in the ability to convert √é¬±-ketoglutarate (√é¬±KG) into the oncometabolite R2-hydroxyglutarate (R2-HG), which can competitively inhibit multiple √é¬±KG-dependent dioxygenases. "],"i":"up-regulates quantity"}},{"id":368,"s":96,"t":92,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:20027304"],"mechanism":["phosphorylation"],"residue":["Ser47","Ser27","Thr530"],"sequence":["ADREAASsPAGEPLR","DGPGLERsPGEPGGA","YLSELPPtPLHVSED"],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":["This phosphorylation increased sirt1 nuclear localization"],"annotator":["gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/20027304\">pubmed:20027304</a> Human sirt1 was phosphorylated by jnk1 on three sites: ser27, ser47, and thr530 and this phosphorylation of sirt1 increased its nuclear localization and enzymatic activity. Surprisingly, jnk1 phosphorylation of sirt1 showed substrate specificity resulting in deacetylation of histone h3, but not p53.","<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/20027304\">pubmed:20027304</a>  Human sirt1 was phosphorylated by jnk1 on three sites: ser27, ser47, and thr530 and this phosphorylation of sirt1 increased its nuclear localization and enzymatic activity. Surprisingly, jnk1 phosphorylation of sirt1 showed substrate specificity resulting in deacetylation of histone h3, but not p53."],"i":"up-regulates"}},{"id":371,"s":144,"t":127,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:17289999"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/17289999\">pubmed:17289999</a> Which of the multiple pro-survival proteins that can bind Bax (fig. S15A) can functionally restrain it? Mcl-1 must, because neutralizing Mcl-1 by enforced Noxa expression rendered MEFs containing only Bax (Bak KO cells) sensitive to the Bad BH3 mimetic ABT-737 (Fig. 4A), which inactivates Bcl-2, Bcl-xL, and Bcl-w"],"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":372,"s":144,"t":119,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:17289999"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/17289999\">pubmed:17289999</a> Bax is held in check by mcl1, bcl-2, and either bcl2l1 or bcl2l2, or by all four. They bind a primed conformer of bak or bax"],"i":"down-regulates"}},{"id":373,"s":35,"t":38,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:15526160"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["miannu"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://identifiers.org/pubmed/15526160\">pubmed:15526160</a> C-Kit stimulates rapid and transient tyrosine phosphorylation of JAK2. JAK2 was found to be constitutively associated with c-Kit, with increased association after ligand stimulation of c-Kit"],"i":"up-regulates activity"}},{"id":374,"s":35,"t":48,"v":{"citation":["pubmed:10377264"],"mechanism":["binding"],"residue":[""],"sequence":[""],"cell_data":[""],"tissue_data":[""],"notes":[""],"annotator":["gcesareni"],"sentence":["<a target=\"_blank\" href